1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Here's a frustrating one. You turn on caching, but your bill doesn't move, and your reads keep coming back zero.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Almost always, the prefix isn't matching on the cache.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
This lesson walks through the render order, the byte exact rule, where breakpoints go, the size floors, and the

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
silent mistakes that break caching.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Three terms carry the mechanics. First, the cache control breakpoint.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
That's an explicit marker you drop on a block, and it closes off a cacheable prefix right at that point.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
You get up to four of them per request.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Second, the rendered prefix.

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
That's everything from the very start of your request up to a breakpoint, and it's assembled in one fixed order,

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,000
tools, then system, then messages. Third, the cache key.

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,000
That's the exact rendered bytes of that prefix, and I mean exact.

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
The match is byte for byte, never fuzzy, and it's always prefix only. So a breakpoint marks where?

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
The render order fixes how the prefix is built, and the byte exact key decides whether you actually hit.

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Hold those three together, and the rest follows.

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Let's nail the order, because it's the thing people get backwards.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
The API renders your request in one fixed sequence before it ever looks for a match.

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Tools go first, right at the front of the prefix. The system prompt renders next, sitting after the tools.

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Then the messages render last, in conversation order. That sequence never changes, and it's why

19
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,000
placement matters so much.

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Here's the useful consequence. If you put a single breakpoint on your last system block, you cache

21
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
your tools and your whole system

22
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
prompt together as one chunk, because tools already rendered ahead of system.

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
So the order isn't trivia. It tells you exactly what a given breakpoint will and won't pull into the cache.

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Tools, system, messages, in that order, every time.

25
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
So how do you actually tell the API what to cache?

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,000
You place cache control breakpoints, and you get up to four per request.

27
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
The rule for placement is simple. Put each one where the content stops changing.

28
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Your big stable system prompt and your tools are natural spots, since they rarely move between calls.

29
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Then the design discipline that follows push everything volatile after your final breakpoint.

30
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
The current user turn per request variables.

31
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Anything that shifts, all of it lives past the last mark. Why?

32
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Because a change anywhere inside the prefix

33
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
invalidates the cache from that point on.

34
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
So keep the stable content up front in front of your breakpoints, and keep the changing content behind them.

35
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Stable first, volatile last, and your cache holds.

36
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
This is where caching quietly fails, and none of these throw an error.

37
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
First, volatile text in the system prompt.

38
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Bake in a timestamp, a UUID, or a session ID, and the bytes change on every call, so nothing matches.

39
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Second, unsorted JSON. If you serialize your tools or data with a different key order each time,

40
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
the bytes shift, even though the content is the same.

41
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Third, a shifting tool set. Tools sit at the front of the prefix,

42
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
so adding or reordering one invalidates everything after it.

43
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Fourth, switching models. Caches are model scoped, so moving the same prefix to a different model is a full miss.

44
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Freeze all four, and your cache survives.

45
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Picture your request in two halves.

46
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
The frozen prefix is the stable front, your tools, your system prompt, and the settled conversation history

47
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
that isn't going to change anymore.

48
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:52,000
That half caches once, and then gets reused byte for byte on every later call.

49
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:59,000
The volatile tail is the other half, the newest user turn and whatever per request data you inject.

50
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
That part changes constantly. The whole discipline of caching is keeping those two halves

51
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
separated by your last breakpoint. Frozen stuff in front of it. Volatile stuff after it.

52
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Do that, and the changing tail never reaches back into the cached prefix to invalidate it.

53
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Mix them, and every request touches the prefix, and you lose the hit. Draw that line deliberately.

54
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Now, the piece the name Checkpointing really points at, a conversation that keeps growing.

55
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Early on, your prefix is short. As the chat runs, earlier turns settle and stop changing, and you

56
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:40,000
want those cached, too.

57
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
So you move a breakpoint forward to cover the newly frozen history.

58
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,000
One mechanical limit to know, a breakpoint only looks back about 20 content blocks to find a matching prior entry.

59
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:59,000
In a long, agentic run with lots of tool calls and results, you can blow past 20 blocks between marks.

60
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
When that happens, you add intermediate breakpoints along the way, so each stretch stays within the lookback window.

61
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
The habit is simple. Re-checkpoint the settled history as it grows, so every new turn reuses

62
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
everything that came before it.

63
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
There's a floor you have to clear, and it trips people up.

64
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:25,000
A prefix has to reach a minimum token count before it caches, and that minimum depends on the model.

65
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:35,000
On Opus 4.8, it's 1024 tokens. On some other models, the floor is lower, for example, 512 tokens on Fable 5.

66
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:45,000
That difference bites. The exact same prefix can cache cleanly on Fable 5, and silently refuse to cache on Opus 4.8,

67
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
purely because it clears one floor and not the other. And when you come in under the floor, there's

68
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:52,000
no error to warn you.

69
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
You just see cache creation come back as zero.

70
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:02,000
So size your stable prefix above the floor for the specific model you're calling, and always name

71
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
that model when you reason about it.

72
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Last piece, proving the cache is real. Every response carries usage fields, and the one to watch is cache-read input tokens.

73
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Fire two calls with an identical prefix, and on the second, that number should climb.

74
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,000
If it sits at a steady zero across repeated identical calls,

75
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,000
something is silently invalidating your prefix, and now you know the suspects.

76
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,000
While you're in there, remember, the full prompt size is the sum of your uncached input,

77
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:41,000
your cache creation tokens, and your cache-read tokens, not the input field alone.

78
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
So when reads don't show up, don't assume caching is off.

79
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Go hunt the one byte that keeps changing between calls, the stray timestamp, the reordered JSON,

80
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
the tool that slipped in. Freeze it, and the reads appear right where you expect.

81
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Let's recap. A cache hit lives or dies on a byte-exact prefix, rendered tools, then system, then messages.

82
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:12,000
You mark up to four breakpoints at stable positions, keep volatile content behind them, and clear

83
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
the per-model token floor.

84
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
1024 on Opus 4.8.

85
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:24,000
One thing to watch, a timestamp, unsorted JSON, or a model switch invalidates this silently.

86
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
So verify with cache-read input tokens.

87
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,000
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

